Z||000000||Uaana||05-19-2000||02:34 AM||||O.K. now I've seen all kinds of PIAA and various other lamps. Has anyone tried to mount a set of driving lamps off a later model chev cavalier? they look like they might sit nicely in the corners of a GT front end.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||05-20-2000||09:28 AM||||I'm a light fanatic so try this site if you like the idea tell me.....<P>Sillhouette.com without the www<P>Next will be an installation of driving lights were the signal is to cover up that hole and instead of the signal I'll use a neon strip glued to the bumper like the BMW Z5.<P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||05-20-2000||12:59 PM||||Mounting driving lights in the TS holes on a GT works very well.  The brackets go right onto the TS mounts, solid metal to metal connection, lights stay aligned and don't vibrate.<BR>Neon turn signals! wish I had read this before I made the driving lights blink!  Hindsight sure makes things obvious.<BR>The 'red orange red orange' blinks look kinda dumb, and Theogre suspects the life of the lights is going to be considerably shortened.<P>I read all the threads regular, but then go and do things the hard way anyhow. No wonder my kids look at me and just shake their heads.||||reg||12
Z||000003||Uaana||05-21-2000||02:32 PM||||Sillhouette.<BR>How goes the testing of the new ecm chips? <BR>I have an 87 GT 2.8 auto. Do you have any numbers yet or still in the testing phase?||||reg||5
Z||000004||2birds||05-21-2000||10:28 PM||||Uaana,<P>I used some small, round Blazers from Wally World, a little pricey at $50, considering they are standard 55 Watt H3 bulbs, but the housing is small enough to just fit in the GT/SE opening, and the mounting bracket is perfect for attaching them to the metal cross piece in there.  They are DOT legal, fog (yellow) light, but the lenses are clear, and the housings are black aluminum (until the heat burns the paint off).  The light is actually useful, too, unlike most OEM fog/driving lights seem to be.  The kit comes with a ricey toggle switch, but no relay. <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 05-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||phillip||05-22-2000||06:58 AM||||when i got my indy hood scoop i saw some tiny driving lights that looked like they'd fit in side the scoop. they didn't, but i've been working on some housings to fit them on like part of the scoop. might look kinda weird but it'll be different. my main concern is that i thought that i've heard that aux. lights can only be mounted so high for street use. like the light bars on trucks can only be used off road. but i'm thinking the car sits so low (and will eventually be about an inch lower) that lights on the roof won't be much higher than lights on a lifted 4x4 anyway. since it blocks my 3rd brake light i also want to cut one of those led strips into it. i think i can do it and keep the scoop funtional for fresh air.||||reg||1
